export const CONTENT: Record<string, string> = {
  "axes/EI.md":
    "status: approved\n\n# Extraversion and Introversion\n\n## What this scale measures\n\nThis scale measures where you get your energy and how much you seek out company, talk and attention.\n\n## Leaning E\n\nYour answers leaned toward Extraversion. You are likely to feel more awake around people, to start conversations and to be at ease with attention on you. A long quiet day alone is more likely to leave you flat than rested.\n\n## Leaning I\n\nYour answers leaned toward Introversion. You are likely to recover your energy in quiet, to say less in groups and to prefer staying out of the spotlight. It describes where your energy goes. It does not mean you dislike people or lack social skill.\n\n## Near the middle\n\nYou probably act either way depending on the day, the people and how tired you are.\n\n## Big Five\n\nThis scale uses questions written for the Big Five trait Extraversion. A result toward E matches higher Extraversion. A result toward I matches lower Extraversion. Researchers describe Extraversion as a continuous trait, with most people near the middle and fewer at either end.\n",
  "axes/JP.md":
    "status: approved\n\n# Judging and Perceiving\n\n## What this scale measures\n\nThis scale measures how you like to run your time and surroundings: with plans, order and things settled, or loosely, with options left open.\n\n## Leaning J\n\nYour answers leaned toward Judging. You are likely to make a plan and follow it, to keep things in order and to feel better once a decision is made. The name is historical. It does not mean you are judgmental.\n\n## Leaning P\n\nYour answers leaned toward Perceiving. You are likely to improvise, to put off closing a decision and to be untroubled by some disorder. The name is historical. It does not mean you are more perceptive than other people.\n\n## Near the middle\n\nYou probably plan some parts of your life and leave others loose.\n\n## Big Five\n\nThis scale uses questions written for the Big Five trait Conscientiousness, mostly its orderliness and self-discipline parts, plus two questions we wrote ourselves for the Perceiving side. A result toward J matches higher Conscientiousness. A result toward P matches lower Conscientiousness. In the research, the link between this type scale and its Big Five trait is weaker than for Extraversion and Openness, so read this scale with a little more caution.\n",
  "axes/SN.md":
    "status: approved\n\n# Sensing and Intuition\n\n## What this scale measures\n\nThis scale measures what kind of information you are drawn to: facts, details and known methods, or ideas, patterns and imagination.\n\n## Leaning S\n\nYour answers leaned toward Sensing. You are likely to trust what you can see, measure and test, to prefer methods you know, and to have limited patience for abstract talk. It describes what you prefer to pay attention to. It says nothing about how well you think.\n\n## Leaning N\n\nYour answers leaned toward Intuition. You are likely to enjoy ideas for their own sake, to daydream, and to look for the pattern behind the facts. It describes what you prefer to pay attention to. It says nothing about how well you think.\n\n## Near the middle\n\nYou probably move between the practical and the abstract depending on the task.\n\n## Big Five\n\nThis scale uses questions written for the Big Five trait Openness to experience, mostly its imagination, intellect and adventurousness parts. A result toward N matches higher Openness. A result toward S matches lower Openness. Lower Openness means a preference for the familiar and the concrete. It is not a measure of intelligence.\n",
  "axes/TF.md":
    "status: approved\n\n# Thinking and Feeling\n\n## What this scale measures\n\nThis scale measures what you put first when you decide and when you deal with people: logic and frankness, or sympathy and cooperation.\n\n## Leaning T\n\nYour answers leaned toward Thinking. You are likely to look at the logic of a problem first, to give honest criticism and to be comfortable disagreeing. It does not mean you have no feelings or do not care about people. It describes what you reach for first.\n\n## Leaning F\n\nYour answers leaned toward Feeling. You are likely to notice how others feel, to weigh the effect on people when you decide and to prefer cooperation over competition. It does not mean you cannot reason. It describes what you reach for first.\n\n## Near the middle\n\nYou probably weigh logic and people about equally, or switch depending on what is at stake.\n\n## Big Five\n\nThis scale uses questions written for the Big Five trait Agreeableness, plus four questions we wrote ourselves for the Thinking side. A result toward F matches higher Agreeableness. A result toward T matches lower Agreeableness. Agreeableness lines up with this scale less closely than Extraversion and Openness line up with theirs, so read this result as a rougher guide than E/I or S/N.\n",
  "big-five/aspects/assertiveness.md":
    "status: draft\n\n# Assertiveness\n\n## What this aspect measures\n\nThis aspect measures how much you take charge, speak up, try to influence others and act first in a group.\n\n## Higher\n\nYour answers leaned toward the higher end. You are likely to say what you think in a group, take the lead when nobody else does and try to persuade people. You tend to act first and to be comfortable when others follow your direction.\n\n## Lower\n\nYour answers leaned toward the lower end. You are likely to let others take the lead and to share your view when asked rather than push it forward. You tend to prefer influencing quietly, or not at all, over directing a group. You may hold back in a meeting even when you have something to say.\n",
  "big-five/aspects/compassion.md":
    "status: draft\n\n# Compassion\n\n## What this aspect measures\n\nThis aspect measures how much you care about other people's feelings and needs, and how much you want to help.\n\n## Higher\n\nYour answers leaned toward the higher end. You are likely to notice when someone is upset, feel their distress and want to help. You tend to make time for people's problems and to weigh their feelings when you decide something. Other people's hardship can stay with you.\n\n## Lower\n\nYour answers leaned toward the lower end. You are likely to keep some emotional distance from other people's problems. You tend to weigh practical facts over feelings when you decide something, and you may expect people to handle their own troubles. This does not mean you are unkind. Feelings are just not your first focus.\n",
  "big-five/aspects/enthusiasm.md":
    "status: draft\n\n# Enthusiasm\n\n## What this aspect measures\n\nThis aspect measures how warm and outgoing you are, how easily you make friends and how often you feel and show positive emotions such as joy and excitement.\n\n## Higher\n\nYour answers leaned toward the higher end. You are likely to make friends easily, show your feelings openly and feel cheerful or excited often. You tend to warm to new people quickly and to enjoy being around others. People can usually tell how you feel.\n\n## Lower\n\nYour answers leaned toward the lower end. You are likely to take time to warm up to new people and to keep your feelings to yourself. Your good moods tend to be quiet rather than excited. You may have a few close friends and feel no need for more.\n",
  "big-five/aspects/industriousness.md":
    "status: draft\n\n# Industriousness\n\n## What this aspect measures\n\nThis aspect measures how readily you get started on tasks, keep your focus and see them through to the end.\n\n## Higher\n\nYour answers leaned toward the higher end. You are likely to start tasks without much delay, keep working when they get dull and finish what you begin. You tend to set goals and push toward them steadily. Wasted time may bother you more than hard work does.\n\n## Lower\n\nYour answers leaned toward the lower end. You are likely to put off tasks that do not interest you and to work in bursts rather than at a steady pace. Your focus may drift, and you may leave some things half done when something new comes along. You tend to work hardest on what you care about.\n",
  "big-five/aspects/intellect.md":
    "status: draft\n\n# Intellect\n\n## What this aspect measures\n\nThis aspect measures how much you enjoy and seek out ideas, such as reading, abstract thinking and puzzles, not how smart you are.\n\n## Higher\n\nYour answers leaned toward the higher end. You are likely to enjoy thinking about abstract ideas, to read for the pleasure of learning and to take on hard questions for their own sake. A problem without an obvious answer is more likely to draw you in than to put you off.\n\n## Lower\n\nYour answers leaned toward the lower end. You are likely to prefer concrete, practical matters over abstract ideas, and to read or study for a clear purpose rather than for its own sake. Long debates about theory may hold little interest for you. You tend to want to know what an idea is for.\n",
  "big-five/aspects/openness.md":
    "status: draft\n\n# Openness\n\n## What this aspect measures\n\nThis aspect measures how much you respond to beauty, art, music, nature and imagination, and how much attention you give to your inner experience.\n\n## Higher\n\nYour answers leaned toward the higher end. You are likely to be moved by music, art or a view, to daydream and to notice your own feelings and impressions in detail. You may seek out new sights, sounds and experiences for their own sake.\n\n## Lower\n\nYour answers leaned toward the lower end. You are likely to focus on the world as it is rather than on daydreams or impressions. Art and music may be things you enjoy casually rather than things that move you deeply. You tend to stay grounded in practical, present concerns.\n",
  "big-five/aspects/orderliness.md":
    "status: draft\n\n# Orderliness\n\n## What this aspect measures\n\nThis aspect measures how much you like order, routine, tidiness, attention to detail and following rules.\n\n## Higher\n\nYour answers leaned toward the higher end. You are likely to keep your things in their place, follow a routine and notice small details. You tend to like clear rules and a set way of doing things. Mess and clutter may bother you until you put them right.\n\n## Lower\n\nYour answers leaned toward the lower end. You are likely to be comfortable with some mess and to change your routine as you go. Details and rules may matter less to you than the overall result. You tend to prefer a loose structure that leaves room to improvise.\n",
  "big-five/aspects/politeness.md":
    "status: draft\n\n# Politeness\n\n## What this aspect measures\n\nThis aspect measures how much you show respect, hold back from pushing your own way and avoid conflict and taking advantage of others.\n\n## Higher\n\nYour answers leaned toward the higher end. You are likely to respect rules and other people's wishes, avoid arguments and hold back from pushing your own interests. You tend to feel uneasy using people to get what you want. You may give way to keep the peace.\n\n## Lower\n\nYour answers leaned toward the lower end. You are likely to challenge people, including those in charge, and to argue for what you want. You tend to say blunt things when you think they need saying, and you may enjoy a debate. Manners matter less to you than saying what you think.\n",
  "big-five/aspects/volatility.md":
    "status: draft\n\n# Volatility\n\n## What this aspect measures\n\nThis aspect measures how easily you get angry or irritated, how quickly your mood changes and how often you lose your composure.\n\n## Higher\n\nYour answers leaned toward the higher end. You are likely to feel irritation or anger quickly and strongly, and your mood can change fast. Small frustrations may set you off, and you may say things in the moment that you later regret. Your feelings usually show.\n\n## Lower\n\nYour answers leaned toward the lower end. You are likely to stay even-tempered and slow to anger. Your mood tends to stay steady through the day, and you usually keep your composure when something goes wrong. Irritation tends to pass quickly without being said out loud.\n",
  "big-five/aspects/withdrawal.md":
    "status: draft\n\n# Withdrawal\n\n## What this aspect measures\n\nThis aspect measures how much you worry, doubt yourself, feel threatened or overwhelmed, and feel discouraged or low.\n\n## Higher\n\nYour answers leaned toward the higher end. You are likely to worry about what might go wrong, doubt your choices and feel overwhelmed when demands pile up. Setbacks may leave you discouraged for a while. This describes how you tend to feel. It is not a diagnosis.\n\n## Lower\n\nYour answers leaned toward the lower end. You are likely to feel at ease with yourself and to stay hopeful when things go wrong. Worry and self-doubt tend not to take hold, and you usually feel able to cope with what comes. Setbacks are likely to pass without leaving you discouraged.\n",
  "big-five/pairs/A.md":
    "status: draft\n\n# Agreeableness, together\n\n## Alike\n\nYour scores are close. You are likely to handle disagreements in a similar way and to expect a similar amount of give and take. If you both lean higher, you are likely to be gentle with each other, and the risk is that a hard subject never gets raised. If you both lean lower, you are likely to speak frankly, and the risk is that arguments get sharp and nobody softens first.\n\n## Different\n\nYour scores are far apart. One of you is likely to give way to keep the peace and to notice feelings first, while the other is more likely to speak bluntly and stand firm. This tends to show up in arguments and in dealing with family, friends or a landlord. Frankness may hurt the gentler one, and tact may look evasive to the blunter one. Neither way is better.\n\n## Questions to talk through\n\n- When we disagree, does each of us feel free to say what we really think?\n- How does each of us prefer to hear criticism?\n- Is there something one of us has been agreeing to only to keep the peace?\n",
  "big-five/pairs/C.md":
    "status: draft\n\n# Conscientiousness, together\n\n## Alike\n\nYour scores are close. You are likely to agree on how tidy the home should be, how far ahead to plan and how strictly to keep to a budget or a schedule. Day to day, that tends to mean fewer arguments about chores and lateness. If you both lean higher, the risk is that rest gets planned away. If you both lean lower, bills and tasks may slip for both of you.\n\n## Different\n\nYour scores are far apart. One of you likely plans ahead, keeps things in order and finishes tasks early, while the other prefers to decide as things come up. This tends to show up in housework, money, travel plans and being on time. The planner may feel they carry the load, and the flexible one may feel watched. Neither way is better. Each of you covers what the other misses.\n\n## Questions to talk through\n\n- Which tasks does each of us care most about getting done on time, and which can wait?\n- How do we split chores and paperwork so neither of us feels they carry the load?\n- How much of a weekend does each of us want planned in advance?\n",
  "big-five/pairs/E.md":
    "status: draft\n\n# Extraversion, together\n\n## Alike\n\nYour scores are close. You are likely to want a similar amount of social time and a similar pace of activity. Day to day, you may agree easily on how many evenings to spend with other people and how long to stay. If you both lean higher, the risk is a full calendar with little time for just the two of you. If you both lean lower, you may see fewer people than either of you would like.\n\n## Different\n\nYour scores are far apart. One of you likely gains energy from people and activity, while the other needs quiet to recover. This tends to show up in evenings, weekends and how long you stay at a gathering. After a disagreement, one of you may want to talk right away while the other needs time first. Neither need is a comment on the relationship, and neither way is better.\n\n## Questions to talk through\n\n- After a hard day, what helps each of us most: company or time alone?\n- How many evenings a week with other people feels right to each of us?\n- What signal can we use at a gathering to say \"I am ready to leave\"?\n",
  "big-five/pairs/N.md":
    "status: draft\n\n# Neuroticism, together\n\n## Alike\n\nYour scores are close. You are likely to react to stress with similar strength, so each of you may find the other's moods easy to understand. If you both lean higher, worry and irritation can build between you, so it helps to agree on how to calm things down. In studies of couples, being alike in personality predicts relationship satisfaction only weakly. How each of you handles negative emotions tends to matter more.\n\n## Different\n\nYour scores are far apart. One of you likely feels worry, irritation and sadness more strongly, while the other stays calmer. The calmer one can help steady things, but may also seem not to take a worry seriously. Neither of you is wrong. In studies of couples, personality similarity overall predicts relationship satisfaction only weakly. How each person handles negative emotions tends to matter more for how satisfied you both feel.\n\n## Questions to talk through\n\n- When one of us is worried or upset, what helps most: reassurance, a plan or just listening?\n- What does each of us do when we feel irritated, and how can the other tell?\n- How can we calm things down when stress starts to build between us?\n",
  "big-five/pairs/O.md":
    "status: draft\n\n# Openness to experience, together\n\n## Alike\n\nYour scores are close. You are likely to want a similar amount of novelty, so choices about travel, food, books and free time come more easily. Day to day, that means fewer disagreements about whether to try something new. If you both lean higher, the risk is chasing the next new thing and losing time for what you already share. If you both lean lower, the risk is a routine that neither of you thinks to change.\n\n## Different\n\nYour scores are far apart. One of you is likely to want new places, ideas and experiences, while the other prefers what is familiar and known. This tends to show up in holidays, weekend plans and conversation: one of you wants to explore a big idea, the other wants to settle the practical question. Neither way is better. One of you brings fresh ideas and the other brings steadiness.\n\n## Questions to talk through\n\n- How often does each of us want to try something new, and how often do we want the familiar?\n- When one of us wants to talk about a big idea, how can the other say whether they have the energy for it?\n- What is one new thing we could try together, and one routine we both want to keep?\n",
  "big-five/traits/A.md":
    "status: draft\n\n# Agreeableness\n\n## What this trait measures\n\nThis trait measures how much you attend to other people's feelings and needs, and how much you avoid conflict and pushiness. It has two parts: Compassion, which is about caring for others, and Politeness, which is about respect and restraint.\n\n## Higher\n\nYour answers leaned toward the higher end. You are likely to notice when someone is upset, to put other people's needs close to your own and to keep the peace when you can. You tend to give people the benefit of the doubt and to soften criticism.\n\n## Middle\n\nYour answers landed near the middle. You probably care about how people feel and also stand your ground when something matters to you. You may be gentle with people close to you and blunter with people who push you. Whether you give way likely depends on the stakes.\n\n## Lower\n\nYour answers leaned toward the lower end. You are likely to say what you think plainly, to argue your side and to put the task ahead of keeping everyone comfortable. You tend to judge a request on its merits before you agree to it. This does not mean you do not care about people.\n\n## Higher in depth\n\nIn everyday life, you are likely to help when asked, and often before being asked. You may find it hard to say no, and you might agree to plans you would rather skip. Arguments tend to upset you, even when you are not part of them.\n\nAt work, you are likely to be a cooperative teammate who notices when a colleague is struggling. You tend to share credit and to avoid pushing your own interests hard. Asking for something for yourself, or giving blunt feedback, may feel uncomfortable.\n\nWith other people, you tend to be warm, forgiving and easy to get along with. People are likely to come to you with their problems. It can help to notice when your own needs are going unmet, and to say so before resentment builds.\n\n## Middle in depth\n\nIn everyday life, you probably help people readily but also keep track of your own needs. You may let small things go and speak up about larger ones. Some arguments seem worth having to you, and you avoid the rest.\n\nAt work, you are likely to cooperate by default and push back when a decision seems unfair or wrong. You can usually give feedback with some care and still make the point. You may be more accommodating with some colleagues and firmer with others.\n\nWith other people, you tend to be friendly and also willing to disagree. Close friends may see your soft side more than strangers do. You may sometimes be unsure whether to keep the peace or raise a problem, and your choice is likely to depend on how much the issue matters to you.\n\n## Lower in depth\n\nIn everyday life, you are likely to be direct about what you want and what you think. You may be skeptical of people's motives until they earn your trust. You tend not to be swayed by guilt or by requests you see as unreasonable.\n\nAt work, you are likely to argue for your position, push back on weak ideas and negotiate firmly. You tend to focus on getting the task right over keeping the mood pleasant. Decisions that need someone to say an unpopular thing may come easier to you.\n\nWith other people, you tend to be frank, and some people value knowing exactly where they stand with you. Others may hear your directness as harsh or uncaring when you did not mean it that way. Saying what you appreciate, as well as what you disagree with, can help.\n",
  "big-five/traits/C.md":
    "status: draft\n\n# Conscientiousness\n\n## What this trait measures\n\nThis trait measures how much you organize your life, keep to plans and follow through on what you start. It has two parts: Industriousness, which is about getting work done, and Orderliness, which is about order and routine.\n\n## Higher\n\nYour answers leaned toward the higher end. You are likely to plan ahead, keep your things and your time in order, and finish what you start. Loose ends and missed deadlines tend to bother you, and you may hold yourself to a stricter standard than anyone asked for.\n\n## Middle\n\nYour answers landed near the middle. You probably plan when it matters and let things slide when it does not. Some parts of your life may be well ordered while others stay loose. How much you follow through likely depends on how much the task means to you.\n\n## Lower\n\nYour answers leaned toward the lower end. You are likely to prefer keeping things open and flexible over fixed plans and routines. You tend to work in bursts when something catches your interest, and a messy desk or a loose schedule is unlikely to bother you much.\n\n## Higher in depth\n\nIn everyday life, you are likely to keep lists, use a calendar and know where your things are. You tend to deal with a chore soon rather than let it build up. Plans that change at the last minute are likely to unsettle you.\n\nAt work, you tend to start on time, keep a steady effort and check your work before you hand it in. People are likely to trust you with tasks that have deadlines. You may find it hard to stop before something is complete, or to accept \"good enough\".\n\nWith other people, you are likely to keep your promises and to expect the same in return. Lateness and broken plans can annoy you. People who live more loosely may see you as rigid, so it can help to say plainly which plans matter to you and which can bend.\n\n## Middle in depth\n\nIn everyday life, you probably keep order where it counts to you, such as bills or appointments, and let other areas go. You may make a plan, follow part of it and adjust the rest as you go. A messy room can sit for a while before it bothers you.\n\nAt work, you are likely to meet the deadlines that matter and to work steadily on tasks you care about. You may put off dull tasks until they become urgent. You can usually work with a strict process or without one.\n\nWith other people, you are likely to be reliable about what you have promised and relaxed about small plans. You may find that you are the organized one with some friends and the easygoing one with others. It can help to tell people which of your plans are firm and which are loose.\n\n## Lower in depth\n\nIn everyday life, you are likely to decide things as they come rather than plan far ahead. Your space may be cluttered without it getting in your way. You tend to change course easily when something better turns up.\n\nAt work, you are likely to work best on tasks that hold your interest, often in bursts close to a deadline. Strict procedures, detailed checklists and long stretches of routine may wear on you. You tend to be comfortable when plans shift at the last minute.\n\nWith other people, you are likely to be easygoing about lateness, mess and changed plans, in yourself and in others. People who rely on fixed plans may read this as not caring. Saying clearly which commitments you will hold to can help them trust the flexible ones.\n",
  "big-five/traits/E.md":
    "status: draft\n\n# Extraversion\n\n## What this trait measures\n\nThis trait measures how much you seek out people, activity and attention, and how often you feel upbeat, energetic emotions. It has two parts: Enthusiasm, which is about warmth and positive emotion, and Assertiveness, which is about taking charge and speaking up.\n\n## Higher\n\nYour answers leaned toward the higher end. You are likely to feel more awake around people, to start conversations easily and to speak up in groups. Good moods tend to come quickly and show. A long day alone is more likely to leave you restless than rested.\n\n## Middle\n\nYour answers landed near the middle. You probably enjoy company and also need time alone, and the balance shifts from day to day. You may speak up in a small group you know well and stay quieter in a large group of strangers.\n\n## Lower\n\nYour answers leaned toward the lower end. You are likely to recover your energy in quiet, to prefer a few close people over a crowd and to stay out of the spotlight. This describes where your energy goes. It does not mean you dislike people.\n\n## Higher in depth\n\nIn everyday life, you are likely to fill your free time with plans, calls and gatherings. You may think out loud and feel better after talking a problem through with someone. Excitement and good moods tend to come easily and show on your face.\n\nAt work, you are likely to enjoy meetings, team tasks and work that puts you in front of people. You may be the first to speak, to offer an opinion or to take the lead. Long hours of quiet work on your own may drain you.\n\nWith other people, you tend to make friends quickly and keep a wide circle. Your energy can lift a room. Quieter people may find it hard to get a word in unless you pause and ask them, and they may need more time alone than you expect.\n\n## Middle in depth\n\nIn everyday life, you probably like a mix of social plans and quiet evenings. A busy weekend may leave you wanting a calm one, and a quiet week may leave you wanting company. You may be talkative with close friends and reserved with new people.\n\nAt work, you are likely to be comfortable both in meetings and in long stretches of focused work alone. You may take the lead when a situation calls for it without seeking the role.\n\nWith other people, you can usually match the energy of the group: lively when others are lively, calm when they are calm. Friends who are more outgoing and friends who are more reserved may both find you easy to be around. It can help to notice when you have had enough of company, or of solitude, and to say so.\n\n## Lower in depth\n\nIn everyday life, you are likely to prefer a quiet evening, a small dinner or time on your own to a large party. Your good moods are likely to be calm rather than excited. After a busy social day, you are likely to need time alone to feel like yourself again.\n\nAt work, you are likely to do your best thinking alone or with one or two people. You may prefer writing to speaking up in a large meeting, and you tend to think before you talk. Roles that need constant contact with strangers may tire you.\n\nWith other people, you tend to keep a small circle of close friends. You are likely to listen more than you talk. Others may read your quiet as distance, so it can help to tell them when you are simply recharging.\n",
  "big-five/traits/N.md":
    "status: draft\n\n# Neuroticism\n\n## What this trait measures\n\nThis trait measures how strongly and how often you feel negative emotions such as worry, irritation and sadness. It has two parts: Volatility, which is about anger and quick changes of mood, and Withdrawal, which is about worry, doubt and low mood.\n\n## Higher\n\nYour answers leaned toward the higher end. You are likely to feel worry, irritation or sadness strongly, and for longer, when things go wrong. You may notice risks early and take setbacks to heart. This describes a personality trait. It is not a diagnosis of anything.\n\n## Middle\n\nYour answers landed near the middle. You probably feel stress, irritation or worry when there is a clear reason, and settle again once it passes. A hard week may unsettle you, but small setbacks usually do not stay with you for long.\n\n## Lower\n\nYour answers leaned toward the lower end. You are likely to stay calm and steady under pressure and to recover quickly from upsets. Worry and irritation tend to pass without taking hold. You may be surprised by how much the same event bothers someone else.\n\n## Higher in depth\n\nIn everyday life, you are likely to feel things strongly. A small criticism or a change of plans can stay on your mind for hours. You may think through what could go wrong before it happens, which can leave you well prepared and also tired from worrying.\n\nAt work, you are likely to care a lot about mistakes and about what others think of your work. Pressure, conflict and uncertain situations may feel heavy. Clear expectations and some warning before changes tend to help.\n\nWith other people, you may want more reassurance, and you may react quickly when you feel let down. You are also likely to notice signs of tension early. People close to you can help by staying steady and saying plainly where things stand. If strong feelings are making daily life hard, talking with someone you trust can help.\n\n## Middle in depth\n\nIn everyday life, you probably feel stress when there is a real reason and settle again once it passes. A bad day can get to you, but you usually feel better by the next morning. Some situations, such as conflict or uncertainty, may affect you more than others.\n\nAt work, you are likely to stay fairly steady under normal pressure and to feel the strain when deadlines pile up or a problem drags on. You may worry about a mistake for a while, then move on.\n\nWith other people, you are likely to show some feeling when you are hurt or annoyed, and then let it go. Strong reactions can happen but are unlikely to last long. You may be the calm one in some relationships and the more anxious one in others, depending on the person.\n\n## Lower in depth\n\nIn everyday life, you are likely to stay even-tempered when things go wrong. Delays, small losses and changed plans tend not to upset you for long. You probably spend little time worrying about what might happen.\n\nAt work, you are likely to stay calm under pressure and to keep working through setbacks. Criticism tends to feel like information rather than a blow. You may be the person others look to when a situation is tense.\n\nWith other people, you tend to be steady and slow to anger. Your calm can help others settle. People who feel things more strongly may read your calm as not caring, or feel that you play down a problem that worries them. Asking how much something matters to them, before you offer reassurance, can help.\n",
  "big-five/traits/O.md":
    "status: draft\n\n# Openness to experience\n\n## What this trait measures\n\nThis trait measures how much you seek out and enjoy new ideas, art, imagination and unfamiliar experiences. It has two parts: Intellect, which is about ideas, and Openness, which is about beauty, feeling and imagination.\n\n## Higher\n\nYour answers leaned toward the higher end. You are likely to enjoy abstract ideas, to notice art, music and nature, and to go looking for things you have not tried. A routine with nothing new in it may start to feel flat to you after a while.\n\n## Middle\n\nYour answers landed near the middle. You probably enjoy some new ideas and experiences, but you also value the familiar and do not go out of your way for novelty. Whether you reach for something new likely depends on the subject, your mood and how much time you have.\n\n## Lower\n\nYour answers leaned toward the lower end. You are likely to prefer the practical and familiar over the abstract and untried. You tend to judge an idea by whether it is useful now, and long talk about theory may interest you less than getting something done.\n\n## Higher in depth\n\nIn everyday life, you are likely to read widely, follow a topic a long way and keep a list of places, foods or music you want to try. Art, a view or a piece of music can move you strongly. You may spend a good part of the day in your own thoughts.\n\nAt work, you tend to enjoy problems without a set answer and tasks where you can try a new approach. Repeating the same steps with no room to change them is likely to wear on you. You may lose interest once a problem is solved and the rest is routine.\n\nWith other people, you tend to enjoy long conversations about ideas, beliefs and what could be. Talk that stays on practical matters may hold your interest less. People who prefer the familiar may find your steady appetite for change tiring.\n\n## Middle in depth\n\nIn everyday life, you probably mix the familiar with the new. You might keep the same favorite places and meals but try something different now and then, often when someone else suggests it. You enjoy some art, music or ideas without making them the center of your free time.\n\nAt work, you are likely to be comfortable with both routine tasks and problems that need a fresh approach. You may prefer new methods that have already been tried somewhere else.\n\nWith other people, you can usually follow a conversation about abstract ideas and also enjoy one about everyday plans. You may act as a bridge between a friend who always wants something new and one who prefers what they know. Your interest in novelty is likely to rise and fall with the topic.\n\n## Lower in depth\n\nIn everyday life, you are likely to know what you like and to stick with it. You may return to the same places, meals and music because they reliably please you. You tend to prefer concrete facts and plain explanations over theories and symbols.\n\nAt work, you are likely to be at ease with clear methods that are known to work. You tend to ask how a new idea will help in practice before you take it up. Changes made for their own sake may frustrate you.\n\nWith other people, you tend to prefer talk about real events and practical plans over long debates about ideas. Your tastes are likely to stay steady, which people close to you can count on. People who chase novelty may read you as cautious, and you may read them as restless.\n",
  "pairs/EI.md":
    "status: approved\n\n# Extraversion and Introversion, together\n\n## Both E\n\nYou both lean toward company and activity. You are likely to agree easily on going out, having people over and talking things through as they happen. Day to day, the risk is a full calendar with little quiet time for the two of you, and two people talking with nobody listening.\n\n## Both I\n\nYou both lean toward quiet. You are likely to give each other space without being asked and to be comfortable in silence together. Day to day, the risk is that neither of you raises a problem, and that you see fewer other people than either of you would like.\n\n## One E, one I\n\nOne of you gains energy from people and the other from quiet. This tends to show up in how you spend evenings and weekends, how long you stay at gatherings and how soon you talk after a disagreement. One of you may want to talk right away while the other needs time first. Neither need is a comment on the relationship.\n\n## Questions to talk through\n\n- After a hard day, what helps each of you most: company or time alone?\n- How many evenings a week with other people feels right to each of you?\n- When we disagree, how long does each of us need before talking about it?\n- What is a signal we can use at a gathering to say \"I am ready to leave\"?\n",
  "pairs/JP.md":
    "status: approved\n\n# Judging and Perceiving, together\n\n## Both J\n\nYou both lean toward plans and order. You are likely to agree on schedules, tidiness and getting decisions made. Day to day, the risk is two firm plans that do not match, and little room for doing something on the spur of the moment.\n\n## Both P\n\nYou both lean toward flexibility. You are likely to give each other freedom and to change plans without friction. Day to day, the risk is that decisions, chores and deadlines get put off by both of you until they become urgent.\n\n## One J, one P\n\nOne of you likes things settled and the other likes options open. This tends to show up in housework, timekeeping, travel and how early you decide things. One may feel left waiting on a decision, and the other may feel pressed to decide too soon. A different pace is not a lack of care on either side.\n\n## Questions to talk through\n\n- Which decisions need to be made early for one of us to relax, and which can stay open?\n- What level of tidiness can we both live with at home?\n- How much of a holiday or weekend should be planned ahead?\n- Which chores does each of us mind least, and can we split them that way?\n",
  "pairs/SN.md":
    "status: approved\n\n# Sensing and Intuition, together\n\n## Both S\n\nYou both lean toward facts and known methods. You are likely to agree on practical matters and to talk in concrete terms about what happened and what to do next. Day to day, the risk is staying with what you know when a change would serve you both, and seldom talking about where things are heading in the long run.\n\n## Both N\n\nYou both lean toward ideas and possibilities. You are likely to enjoy long talks about plans, meanings and what could be. Day to day, the risk is that practical details such as bills, repairs and bookings belong to nobody, and that plans stay as talk.\n\n## One S, one N\n\nOne of you starts from the facts and the other from the idea. This tends to show up in conversation: one wants the specifics, the other wants the point. One may find the other vague, and the other may find the first one stuck on details. Each of you tends to catch what the other misses.\n\n## Questions to talk through\n\n- When we plan something, who looks after the details and who looks after the overall aim? Are we both happy with that?\n- What is one thing we keep doing the same way that one of us would like to change?\n- When I describe a problem, do you want the facts first or the conclusion first?\n- Which practical jobs are falling through the gaps right now?\n",
  "pairs/TF.md":
    "status: approved\n\n# Thinking and Feeling, together\n\n## Both T\n\nYou both lean toward logic and frankness. You are likely to say what you think to each other and to settle a disagreement by arguing it out. Day to day, the risk is that a debate gets won while a hurt goes unnoticed, and that neither of you says the warm thing out loud.\n\n## Both F\n\nYou both lean toward sympathy and cooperation. You are likely to be gentle with each other and quick to notice when the other is upset. Day to day, the risk is that you both avoid a hard subject to keep the peace, and that a needed criticism never gets said.\n\n## One T, one F\n\nOne of you reaches first for the logic and the other for how people are affected. This tends to show up when one of you brings a problem home: one offers a fix, the other wanted to be heard. Frank feedback may sting more than it was meant to, and tact may be read as avoiding the point.\n\n## Questions to talk through\n\n- When I tell you about a problem, how can I show whether I want advice or just to be heard?\n- How does each of us prefer to receive criticism?\n- Is there something we have both been avoiding saying?\n- What does each of us do, in practice, that the other should read as care?\n",
  "privacy.md":
    "status: draft\n\n# Privacy\n\n## What we store on our server\n\nWhen you finish a test we store:\n\n- your answers: 40 for the four-letter test, 100 for the Big Five test\n- your scores: four percentages and your four-letter type, or five trait scores and ten aspect scores\n- the date and time\n- a scrambled copy of your delete code, so we can check it later\n\nWe do not ask for your name or email, and we do not store them. Your result gets a random 16-character ID. Nothing in it identifies you.\n\nWe keep your answers so that we can check how well the questions work and rescore results if we improve the scoring.\n\n## What is stored on your device\n\nYour browser stores:\n\n- your progress while you take the test, so a reload does not lose it. This is removed when you submit.\n- a list of your result IDs and their delete codes, so the site can show \"Your last result\" and let you delete it.\n\nThis stays in your browser. Clearing your browser's site data removes it.\n\n## Who can see your result\n\nAnyone who has the link to your result can see your type and your four percentages, or your five trait scores. They cannot see your individual answers. If you send someone a compare link, they will see your result.\n\nA comparison has its own link. Anyone who has it can see both results in it. The full reports and couple reports open only on a device with the Personality unlock or a PolymadLabs pass. A link alone does not open them.\n\n## If you pay\n\nPayment happens on Stripe's page. Stripe collects your email and card details and sends you a receipt. We never see your card number, and we do not copy your email into our database.\n\nAfter paying you get an unlock code. This browser keeps the code and a random device id, and sends both with each request for a report, so our server can check them.\n\nOn our server we keep one record of the purchase:\n\n- Stripe's reference for the payment, the amount, the currency, whether it is paid, and the date.\n- A scrambled form of your unlock code (a hash). The code itself cannot be read back from it.\n- The random device ids that used the code, with the day each was first and last seen. A code works on up to 5 devices, so it cannot be shared widely.\n- For each day the code is checked, a scrambled form of the network addresses it was checked from, to spot a code used from many places. The addresses themselves are not kept.\n\nPurchases made before November 2026 unlocked one result instead. Their records also say which result they unlocked.\n\n## If you use a PolymadLabs pass\n\nWith a PolymadLabs pass, every full report, the Big Five report and every couple report are unlocked. The test keeps your pass code and a random device id in this browser's storage. When you open a report, our server sends both to the PolymadLabs pass service to check the pass. The pass service keeps the device ids that used each code, with the day each was first seen, so one code cannot be shared widely. The pass privacy note at polymadlabs.com/pass/privacy has the details.\n\n## How long we keep it\n\nWe keep a result until you delete it. Purchase records are kept for accounting and so your unlock code keeps working. Device ids and scrambled addresses are deleted once they have not been seen for a year.\n\n## How to delete your result\n\nOpen your result on the device you took the test on and press \"Delete this result\". It is removed from our server right away.\n\nDeleting needs the delete code saved in your browser. If you cleared your browser data or changed device, the button will not be there.\n\n## What we do not do\n\n- No advertising.\n- No outside analytics or tracking services.\n- We do not sell or share your data.\n\n## Where it is stored\n\nResults are stored in a database run by Cloudflare, the company that hosts this site.\n",
  "science.md":
    "status: draft\n\n# The science\n\n## What this test measures\n\nThis test asks 40 questions and reports four scales. Each scale is built from questions written to measure one of the Big Five personality traits, the model most used in personality research. We report the results with type letters because many people know them.\n\n- E or I comes from questions about Extraversion.\n- S or N comes from questions about Openness to experience.\n- T or F comes from questions about Agreeableness.\n- J or P comes from questions about Conscientiousness.\n\nA 1989 study by McCrae and Costa found that the four type scales line up with these four traits. The link was strong for E/I and S/N and moderate for T/F and J/P. The fifth Big Five trait, Neuroticism, has no type letter and this test does not measure it. The Big Five test below does.\n\n## Why you get percentages\n\nTypes suggest two kinds of people on each scale. The data does not show that. On each trait most people score near the middle and fewer score at the ends. Most people are near the middle on at least one scale.\n\nThat is why we show a percentage for each scale. When your winning side is 55% or less, we label the scale \"balanced\". The letter still appears in your four-letter code, but it tells you little.\n\n## How stable your result is\n\nEach scale has 10 questions. One answer changed by one step moves that scale by 2.5 percentage points. If you are near 50%, one or two different answers on another day can change your letter. Your percentage will have barely moved.\n\nSo a different four-letter code on a retest does not mean you changed. Compare the percentages.\n\n## Where the questions come from\n\nThirty-two questions come from the International Personality Item Pool (IPIP), a public-domain collection used in research. We added \"I\" to the start of each one.\n\nWe wrote the other eight questions ourselves: four for Thinking, two for Sensing and two for Perceiving. The IPIP questions on those sides mostly describe faults instead of preferences. Our eight questions have not been validated yet. We will check how well they work once enough people have taken the test.\n\n## The Big Five test\n\nThe Big Five test is a second, longer test on this site. It asks 100 questions and measures all five traits directly: Openness to experience, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness and Neuroticism. It gives a score from 0 to 100 for each, with no type letters.\n\nThe questions are the Big Five Aspect Scales, published by DeYoung, Quilty and Peterson in 2007. Each trait has 20 questions, split into two aspects of 10. The aspects are a finer level of detail that the authors found in the data: for example, Conscientiousness splits into Industriousness, which is about getting things done, and Orderliness, which is about keeping things in order. In the authors' data the reliability of the trait scales was between 0.84 and 0.89, and of the aspect scales between 0.75 and 0.85.\n\nNinety-six of the 100 questions come from the IPIP. Three are reworded versions of IPIP or Big Five Inventory questions, and one was written by the authors. All 100 are the ones they published. As with the four-letter test, we added \"I\" to the start of each one.\n\nA score shows where your answers sat on that scale: 0 means every answer went toward the low end and 100 every answer toward the high end. It is not a comparison with other people. We do not have enough results to say what is usual yet. Scores from 40 to 60 are shown as near the middle. One answer changed by one step moves a trait by 1.25 points and an aspect by 2.5 points.\n\nA higher Neuroticism score means you tend to feel negative emotions such as worry or irritation more strongly or more often. It is not a diagnosis of anything.\n\n## What this test is not\n\nIt is not a clinical tool. It cannot diagnose anything. It was not built for hiring and should not be used to choose between people. It does not measure intelligence or ability, and it cannot tell you which job or partner is right for you.\n\n## About comparing two people\n\nThe compare page shows how similar two sets of answers are. Research on couples finds that having similar personalities predicts relationship satisfaction only weakly. A large study across three countries found that similarity added very little once each partner's own traits were counted.\n\n## Sources\n\n- McCrae, R. R., and Costa, P. T. (1989). Journal of Personality, 57(1), pages 17 to 40.\n- Goldberg, L. R. (1992). The development of markers for the Big-Five factor structure. Psychological Assessment, 4(1), pages 26 to 42.\n- Goldberg, L. R., and others (2006). The International Personality Item Pool and the future of public-domain personality measures. Journal of Research in Personality, 40(1), pages 84 to 96.\n- Dyrenforth, P. S., Kashy, D. A., Donnellan, M. B., and Lucas, R. E. (2010). Predicting relationship and life satisfaction from personality in nationally representative samples from three countries. Journal of Personality and Social Psychology, 99(4), pages 690 to 702.\n- DeYoung, C. G., Quilty, L. C., and Peterson, J. B. (2007). Between facets and domains: 10 aspects of the Big Five. Journal of Personality and Social Psychology, 93(5), pages 880 to 896.\n- International Personality Item Pool: ipip.ori.org. The Big Five Aspect Scales are listed at ipip.ori.org/BFASKeys.htm.\n",
  "types/ENFJ.md":
    "status: approved\n\n# ENFJ\n\n## Summary\n\nYou prefer company and activity, ideas and possibilities, decisions that consider people, and a clear plan. You tend to see what a person or group could become and to organize people toward it. You are likely to talk easily with most people and to pick up how they feel. You like to turn concern for others into a plan with steps and dates. Disharmony in a group tends to bother you, and you usually move to settle it.\n\n## Strengths\n\nYou tend to bring out effort in other people by showing that you believe in them. You explain an idea in a way that makes people want to take part. You keep a group pointed at its goal and attend to how its members are doing. You usually follow through on what you organize.\n\n## Blind spots\n\nYou may decide what is good for someone without asking them. You can take on too many people's needs and neglect your own. Hard facts that spoil a hopeful picture may get less attention than they should. You may take disagreement as a sign that a relationship is in trouble.\n\n## At work\n\nYou tend to want a shared purpose, people to guide or teach and a clear plan. You are likely to prefer working with a group over working alone. Cynicism, unresolved conflict and work that helps no one tend to drain you. You usually end up in the middle of a team's communication.\n\n## Under stress\n\nYou may overcommit, then become controlling or easily hurt. You might start picking apart your own reasoning and other people's, which is unlike you. Time alone to sort out what is yours to carry, then an honest talk, usually helps.\n\n## How others may read you\n\nPeople may see you as warm, persuasive and sure of the direction. Some may feel managed. Those close to you tend to feel strongly supported. You may need to let people reach their own conclusions, even when you can see a faster route.\n",
  "types/ENFP.md":
    "status: approved\n\n# ENFP\n\n## Summary\n\nYou prefer company and activity, ideas and possibilities, decisions guided by your values, and open options. You tend to get excited by new ideas and to share them out loud with whoever is near. You are likely to connect with people fast and to care how they feel. You see many ways a thing could go and may dislike picking just one. Routine and detailed follow-through tend to hold less interest for you than starting something new.\n\n## Strengths\n\nYou tend to see possibilities in people and situations that others have written off. You bring energy to a new idea and get others interested in it. You make connections between things that seem unrelated. You usually make people feel heard, and they open up to you quickly.\n\n## Blind spots\n\nYou may start more than you can finish. You can say yes to too many people because each request seemed worth it at the time. Details, deadlines and money matters may get left late. You may avoid giving bad news and let a problem run on.\n\n## At work\n\nYou tend to want variety, people to think out loud with and freedom to try things. You are likely to prefer starting projects over maintaining them. Strict routines, close monitoring and working alone for long periods tend to drain you. You usually do your best work when you believe in the purpose.\n\n## Under stress\n\nYou may scatter your effort across too many things and finish none. You might fix on one small detail or worry, which is unlike you. Talking it through with someone, then cutting the list down to one or two things, usually helps.\n\n## How others may read you\n\nPeople may see you as enthusiastic, warm and full of ideas. Some may doubt you will follow through. Those close to you tend to know your interest in them is real and lasting. You may need to under-promise, so that what you deliver matches what you said.\n",
  "types/ENTJ.md":
    "status: approved\n\n# ENTJ\n\n## Summary\n\nYou prefer company and activity, ideas and patterns, decisions made by logic, and a clear plan. You tend to see where a group could go and to start directing it there, out loud. You are likely to set goals, lay out the steps and push for a decision. You say what you think of an idea directly, and you expect others to do the same. Inefficiency and indecision tend to frustrate you. You usually have more energy for a challenge than for keeping things as they are.\n\n## Strengths\n\nYou tend to see the long-range goal and the structure needed to reach it. You make decisions and accept responsibility for them. You speak up when something is not working. You usually keep pushing through obstacles that would stop a plan held less firmly.\n\n## Blind spots\n\nYou may move ahead before others have had time to think or object. You can dismiss a concern because it was raised with feeling instead of argument. Details of how the work gets done day to day may escape you. You may count rest and patience as lost time.\n\n## At work\n\nYou tend to want a large goal, authority to organize toward it and capable people. You are likely to prefer a fast decision that can be corrected over a slow one. Unclear direction, repeated mistakes and people who will not say what they think tend to frustrate you. You usually take the lead when no one else does.\n\n## Under stress\n\nYou may push yourself and others harder and listen less. You might feel suddenly alone or unappreciated, which is unlike you. Stepping back, asking one trusted person for a frank view and taking real time off usually help.\n\n## How others may read you\n\nPeople may see you as decisive, confident and demanding. Some may find you intimidating or impatient. Those who work with you tend to respect that you are clear and consistent. You may need to show that you have heard an objection before you answer it.\n",
  "types/ENTP.md":
    "status: approved\n\n# ENTP\n\n## Summary\n\nYou prefer company and activity, ideas and patterns, decisions made by logic, and open options. You tend to think out loud and to test ideas by arguing them with other people. You are likely to question the usual way of doing things and to enjoy a debate even when you do not hold a firm side. You see many possibilities and may resist closing any of them. Routine, schedules and finishing details tend to hold less interest for you than the next problem.\n\n## Strengths\n\nYou tend to come up with several ways around a problem while others are still describing it. You spot the weak point in an argument quickly. You are at ease changing course when a better idea appears. You usually enjoy being challenged, so people can disagree with you openly.\n\n## Blind spots\n\nYou may argue a point for the exercise and leave the other person feeling attacked. You can lose interest once the hard part is solved and the routine part begins. Commitments and deadlines may slip. You may handle a personal matter as a puzzle when the other person wanted sympathy.\n\n## At work\n\nYou tend to want new problems, quick-thinking people and freedom from fixed procedure. You are likely to prefer designing the approach over carrying out every step. Repetition, slow decisions and rules kept only out of habit tend to frustrate you. You usually have more ideas than time.\n\n## Under stress\n\nYou may jump between tasks, argue more and finish less. You might fix on small details or on how your body feels, which is unlike you. Rest, exercise and choosing one thing to complete usually help.\n\n## How others may read you\n\nPeople may see you as quick, funny and willing to say what others will not. Some may find you tiring or think you argue for the sake of it. Those who know you tend to value that you take their ideas seriously enough to test them. You may need to say when you agree, not only when you object.\n",
  "types/ESFJ.md":
    "status: approved\n\n# ESFJ\n\n## Summary\n\nYou prefer company and activity, concrete facts, decisions that consider people, and a clear plan. You tend to keep track of the people around you: who needs what, who has not been heard from, what was promised. You are likely to organize things so that others are looked after, and to follow through. You value cooperation and established ways of doing things. Conflict and loose ends both tend to bother you. People usually find you warm and easy to approach.\n\n## Strengths\n\nYou tend to bring people together and make sure the practical side is handled. You remember birthdays, preferences and who is going through a hard time. You do what you said you would. You usually notice tension in a group early and act to ease it.\n\n## Blind spots\n\nYou may take on other people's problems until you are overloaded. You can avoid a needed disagreement to keep things pleasant. A break from tradition may feel wrong to you before you have weighed it. You may feel hurt when your effort goes unthanked, without saying so.\n\n## At work\n\nYou tend to want a cooperative team, clear expectations and contact with people through the day. You are likely to prefer agreed routines over constant change. Cold or competitive settings, and criticism given without care, tend to wear on you. You usually keep the team's practical and social life running.\n\n## Under stress\n\nYou may try harder to please everyone and grow resentful when it does not work. You might become unusually critical or start to doubt your own judgment. Talking with someone who appreciates you, and dropping one obligation, usually help.\n\n## How others may read you\n\nPeople may see you as friendly, organized and generous with your time. Some may find your help more than they asked for. Those close to you tend to feel well looked after. You may need to ask what someone wants before you arrange it for them.\n",
  "types/ESFP.md":
    "status: approved\n\n# ESFP\n\n## Summary\n\nYou prefer company and activity, concrete experience, decisions that consider people, and open options. You tend to enjoy what is happening now and to draw others into it. You are likely to be warm, easy to talk to and quick to notice when someone is left out. You learn by doing more than by reading about it. You usually take plans loosely and change them when something better comes up. Long stretches alone or abstract discussion are likely to drain you.\n\n## Strengths\n\nYou tend to put people at ease within minutes. You notice practical things others need and help on the spot. You adapt to a change of plan without fuss. You usually lift the mood of a group, and you make ordinary tasks more pleasant to share.\n\n## Blind spots\n\nYou may put off anything dull or far away, such as paperwork and saving. You can avoid a serious talk by changing the subject. Promises made in a warm moment may be more than you can keep. You may take criticism personally and for longer than you show.\n\n## At work\n\nYou tend to want people around, hands-on tasks and room to move. You are likely to prefer a friendly team over a quiet desk. Long solitary projects, strict routines and cold feedback tend to wear on you. You usually learn a job fastest by being shown once and then trying it.\n\n## Under stress\n\nYou may keep busy to avoid the problem, or look for company and distraction. You might start imagining the worst outcome, which is unlike you. Talking with someone you trust, then picking one small next step, usually helps.\n\n## How others may read you\n\nPeople may see you as cheerful, generous and easy to be around. Some may assume you are never serious. Those close to you tend to know that you feel things strongly and remember kindness. You may need to let people see the worried side as well, so that they can help.\n",
  "types/ESTJ.md":
    "status: approved\n\n# ESTJ\n\n## Summary\n\nYou prefer company and activity, concrete facts, decisions made by logic, and a clear plan. You tend to take charge of getting things organized: who does what, by when, in what order. You trust methods that have been proven, and you say plainly when something is not working. You are likely to finish what you commit to and to expect the same from others. Open-ended discussion with no decision tends to frustrate you. People usually know where they stand with you.\n\n## Strengths\n\nYou tend to turn a goal into steps and get people moving on them. You decide quickly once you have the facts. You hold to your commitments and to the standards you set. You usually say what you expect in plain words, which saves people from guessing.\n\n## Blind spots\n\nYou may decide before everyone has been heard. You can see a different way of working as a wrong way. How people feel about a decision may get little weight next to whether it is efficient. You may find it hard to leave a thing unsettled when waiting would be wiser.\n\n## At work\n\nYou tend to want clear authority, measurable goals and a team that delivers on time. You are likely to prefer a proven process over an experiment. Vague roles, missed deadlines and repeated debate over a settled question tend to frustrate you. You usually take on responsibility without being asked.\n\n## Under stress\n\nYou may take tighter control and become more critical of others. You might feel unappreciated and suddenly take things personally, which is unlike you. Getting the facts down on paper, handing off one task and taking a real break usually help.\n\n## How others may read you\n\nPeople may see you as capable, direct and in charge. Some may find you bossy or quick to judge. Those who work with you tend to trust that you will do what you said. You may need to ask more questions and to give praise as readily as you give corrections.\n",
  "types/ESTP.md":
    "status: approved\n\n# ESTP\n\n## Summary\n\nYou prefer company and activity, concrete facts, decisions made by logic, and open options. You tend to act first and adjust as you go. You notice what is happening in the room and respond to it quickly. You are likely to say what you think plainly and to enjoy some competition. Long plans and theory tend to bore you, and you would sooner try a thing and see. You usually bring energy to a group and get restless when little is happening.\n\n## Strengths\n\nYou tend to move while others are still discussing. You read a situation as it is, not as it was supposed to be. You stay practical under pressure and bargain or improvise your way through. You usually speak directly, so people know what you want.\n\n## Blind spots\n\nYou may act before you have thought about what follows next month. You can be blunt in a way that costs you goodwill. Routine upkeep and long commitments may get dropped once the exciting part is over. You may take a risk for the sake of the action itself.\n\n## At work\n\nYou tend to want variety, quick results and people around you. You are likely to prefer solving a live problem over planning for a possible one. Long meetings, heavy procedure and sitting still tend to wear on you. You usually do your best work when something real is at stake today.\n\n## Under stress\n\nYou may push harder, take bigger risks or look for a distraction. You might become impatient and sharp with people who slow you down. Physical activity, and a short concrete list of what to do next, usually help more than a long talk.\n\n## How others may read you\n\nPeople may see you as confident, fun and direct. Some may read your speed as carelessness and your bluntness as not caring. Those who know you tend to count on you when things go wrong in the moment. You may need to slow down and listen to the end before you answer.\n",
  "types/INFJ.md":
    "status: approved\n\n# INFJ\n\n## Summary\n\nYou prefer quiet, ideas and patterns, decisions that weigh the effect on people, and a clear plan. You tend to think for a long time about what things mean and where they are heading, mostly in private. You care how choices affect others, and you like to act on that with a plan instead of leaving it open. You are likely to have a few close relationships more than many light ones. You may hold strong convictions that few people hear about.\n\n## Strengths\n\nYou tend to see where a situation between people is heading before it is said out loud. You connect an idea to what it would mean for the people involved. When you care about a goal, you organize yourself and keep at it. You usually listen closely and remember what was underneath the words.\n\n## Blind spots\n\nYou may form a picture of how things should go and hold to it after the facts have moved. You can take in other people's feelings until you have little energy left. Practical details may get less attention than the meaning behind them. You may expect others to sense what you have not told them.\n\n## At work\n\nYou tend to want work that fits your values, room to think alone and a clear end point. You are likely to prefer depth on one thing over switching between many. Conflict in a team tends to weigh on you more than it does on others. You usually do your best thinking in writing or after time to reflect.\n\n## Under stress\n\nYou may withdraw, go silent and turn the problem over alone for too long. You might become unusually strict about plans, or suddenly cut off something that has drained you. Time alone, followed by one honest conversation, usually helps more than being cheered up.\n\n## How others may read you\n\nPeople may find you warm but hard to know. You may seem calm while a lot is going on inside. Some are surprised by how firm you are once a value of yours is crossed. You may need to share your thinking earlier, because your conclusions can seem to come from nowhere.\n",
  "types/INFP.md":
    "status: approved\n\n# INFP\n\n## Summary\n\nYou prefer quiet, ideas and possibilities, decisions guided by your values, and open options. You tend to spend a lot of time in your own thoughts, imagining how things could be. You are likely to care strongly about a few people and causes, and to feel the moods of others easily. You usually avoid conflict and competition. Plans, schedules and tidy surroundings tend to matter less to you than staying true to what you care about. You tend to decide late.\n\n## Strengths\n\nYou tend to see what matters to a person underneath what they say. You imagine alternatives that others have not considered. You stay with a cause or a person you believe in without needing a reward. You usually give people room to be themselves, and you listen without rushing to fix.\n\n## Blind spots\n\nYou may start more things than you finish, because the idea held more interest than the routine. You can take criticism of your work as criticism of you. Practical details and deadlines may slip. You may hold an ideal so high that real people and real results keep falling short of it.\n\n## At work\n\nYou tend to want work that means something to you, freedom in how you do it and little conflict. You are likely to prefer working alone or with one or two trusted people. Rigid procedures, close monitoring and work that goes against your values tend to drain you fast. You usually work in bursts of strong interest.\n\n## Under stress\n\nYou may withdraw into your thoughts and judge yourself harshly. You might put everything off, or become unusually sharp and critical. Time alone, something creative and one person who listens without giving advice usually help.\n\n## How others may read you\n\nPeople may see you as gentle, thoughtful and a little hard to pin down. Your quietness can hide how strongly you feel. Some are surprised when you refuse to bend on a matter of principle. You may need to say what you want directly, because hints often go unnoticed.\n",
  "types/INTJ.md":
    "status: approved\n\n# INTJ\n\n## Summary\n\nYou prefer quiet, ideas and patterns, decisions made by logic, and a clear plan. You tend to build a picture of how something should work, then arrange the steps to get there. You are likely to question a rule until you see its reason. You would usually choose an accurate answer over a comfortable one, and you say so. You tend to work best with long stretches alone. Small talk and loose ends are both likely to wear on you.\n\n## Strengths\n\nYou tend to think several steps ahead and to plan for what could go wrong. You judge an idea by whether it holds up, whoever it came from. Once you have a plan you usually carry it out without needing to be pushed. You are comfortable disagreeing with a group when the reasoning points the other way.\n\n## Blind spots\n\nYou may settle on a plan in your head and act as if it were agreed before anyone else has heard it. You can undervalue how people feel about a decision that is logically sound. Facts on the ground that do not fit your model may get explained away. You may come across as certain when you are only being direct.\n\n## At work\n\nYou tend to want a hard problem, the freedom to solve it your way and few interruptions. You are likely to prefer a written argument over a long meeting. Rules with no stated reason, and work redone for the sake of appearances, tend to frustrate you. You usually hold yourself to a higher standard than anyone has asked for.\n\n## Under stress\n\nYou may pull back, work longer hours alone and trust other people's input less. You might grow more critical and more rigid about the plan. Stepping away from the problem and doing something physical usually helps more than thinking harder about it.\n\n## How others may read you\n\nPeople may read you as distant, sure of yourself and hard to impress. Your criticism of an idea can be heard as criticism of the person. Those who work with you longer tend to find you fair and consistent. You may need to say what you appreciate, because you are likely to voice problems first.\n",
  "types/INTP.md":
    "status: approved\n\n# INTP\n\n## Summary\n\nYou prefer quiet, ideas and patterns, decisions made by logic, and open options. You tend to take an idea apart to see whether it holds, and you enjoy that for its own sake. You are likely to question claims, including your own, and to say when an argument has a hole in it. You would usually keep a question open instead of settling it early. Routine, schedules and tidying up tend to hold little interest. You often need long stretches alone to think.\n\n## Strengths\n\nYou tend to find the flaw in a line of reasoning and the pattern under a set of facts. You stay with a hard question long after others have accepted a rough answer. You change your mind when the evidence changes. You usually judge an idea on its merits, whoever said it.\n\n## Blind spots\n\nYou may keep analyzing after it is time to act. You can correct someone's logic when they wanted support. Follow-through on routine tasks, messages and deadlines may slip. You may explain an idea in more detail than the listener asked for and lose them on the way.\n\n## At work\n\nYou tend to want a difficult problem, the freedom to approach it your own way and time to think without interruption. You are likely to prefer a few precise words over a long meeting. Fixed procedures, status reports and decisions made by rank instead of reasons tend to frustrate you. You usually work in uneven bursts.\n\n## Under stress\n\nYou may retreat into your head and stop replying to people. You might put off every decision, or become unusually touchy about being misunderstood. Time alone, sleep and a problem small enough to finish usually help.\n\n## How others may read you\n\nPeople may see you as detached, skeptical and lost in thought. Your questions can sound like disagreement when you are only checking. Those who know you tend to find you open-minded and fair. You may need to tell people you are interested in them, because your attention to ideas is easier to see.\n",
  "types/ISFJ.md":
    "status: approved\n\n# ISFJ\n\n## Summary\n\nYou prefer quiet, concrete facts, decisions that consider people, and a settled plan. You tend to notice what the people around you need and to take care of it without being asked. You remember details about others, such as what they like and what went wrong last time. You trust methods that have worked before. You are likely to keep commitments even when they cost you. You tend to avoid the spotlight and may find it hard to ask for help in return.\n\n## Strengths\n\nYou tend to be dependable in small, practical ways that people come to rely on. You remember what matters to each person and act on it. You follow through on routine work that others let slide. You usually keep the peace by preparing ahead, so that fewer things go wrong in the first place.\n\n## Blind spots\n\nYou may say yes when you mean no, then feel worn down and unseen. You can avoid a hard conversation for so long that the problem grows. A new way of doing things may feel like a risk before you have looked at it. You may wait to be noticed instead of saying what you need.\n\n## At work\n\nYou tend to want a stable team, clear expectations and work that visibly helps someone. You are likely to prefer a known process and time to do things properly. Being rushed, or being asked to deliver criticism in public, is likely to sit badly with you. You usually do more behind the scenes than you report.\n\n## Under stress\n\nYou may keep working harder and say less. Small worries about what could go wrong can multiply, and you might take other people's moods as your fault. Quiet time, a familiar routine and one person who asks how you are usually help.\n\n## How others may read you\n\nPeople may see you as quiet, kind and easy to lean on. Some may assume you have no strong opinions because you do not push them. Those close to you tend to learn that you notice a great deal and remember it. You may need to state your limits plainly, because others will not always guess them.\n",
  "types/ISFP.md":
    "status: approved\n\n# ISFP\n\n## Summary\n\nYou prefer quiet, concrete experience, decisions guided by your values, and open options. You tend to live in what is in front of you: the people, the place, the task at hand. You are likely to be gentle with others and to dislike conflict and competition. You show what matters to you through what you do more than what you say. You tend to take things as they come and to resist being scheduled. You may keep strong feelings private.\n\n## Strengths\n\nYou tend to notice what a person or a moment needs and to respond in a practical way. You accept people as they are and rarely push your views on them. You adjust easily when plans change. You usually put care into how things look, feel or sound, and into doing a thing well for its own sake.\n\n## Blind spots\n\nYou may avoid a disagreement until you quietly leave the situation instead. You can put off decisions and long-term planning because they close off choices. Criticism may hit harder than the other person meant it to. You may assume people know what you value when you have never told them.\n\n## At work\n\nYou tend to want freedom in how you do the work, a calm setting and people who are kind to each other. You are likely to prefer practical tasks with a visible result over theory. Tight schedules, public competition and blunt feedback tend to wear on you. You usually work in your own rhythm.\n\n## Under stress\n\nYou may go quiet, pull away and become hard on yourself. You might drop plans altogether, or turn unusually critical of people you care about. Time alone doing something with your hands, and no demand to decide today, usually help.\n\n## How others may read you\n\nPeople may see you as quiet, easygoing and kind. Some may miss how strongly you hold your values, because you rarely argue for them. Those close to you tend to find you loyal in a low-key way. You may need to speak up earlier, before a small hurt becomes a reason to leave.\n",
  "types/ISTJ.md":
    "status: approved\n\n# ISTJ\n\n## Summary\n\nYou prefer quiet, facts, logic and a plan. You tend to work through things alone and in order, and you trust what has been tested over what merely sounds good. When you decide, you weigh consistency before feelings, and once you commit you usually finish. You are likely to keep your word, keep records and notice when a detail is off. People may find you reserved at first. You tend to show care by being reliable more than by saying it.\n\n## Strengths\n\nYou tend to finish what you start and to do it the way you said you would. You check facts before you rely on them, so your work usually holds up. You are comfortable working alone for long stretches. When rules are clear, you apply them the same way to everyone, including yourself.\n\n## Blind spots\n\nYou may hold on to a method because it has worked before, even after conditions change. You can deliver a correct verdict without noticing how it lands. Ideas with no evidence yet may get dismissed too early. You may take on work quietly instead of saying you are overloaded.\n\n## At work\n\nYou tend to want clear goals, defined roles and enough notice to prepare. You are likely to prefer written instructions over loose talk, and a quiet place over an open room. A sudden change of plan with no reason given is likely to bother you more than a heavy workload does.\n\n## Under stress\n\nYou may tighten your routines and go quiet. You might replay what went wrong in detail, or take over tasks because doing them yourself feels safer than explaining them. Time alone with a concrete task to finish usually helps more than talking it through.\n\n## How others may read you\n\nPeople may read your reserve as coolness and your directness as criticism. Those who know you longer tend to see the steadiness behind both. You may need to say out loud that you care, because the people around you cannot always tell from your actions alone.\n",
  "types/ISTP.md":
    "status: approved\n\n# ISTP\n\n## Summary\n\nYou prefer quiet, concrete facts, decisions made by logic, and open options. You tend to learn how something works by handling it, and you trust what you have seen for yourself. You are likely to stay calm and practical when a problem turns up, and to fix it without much talk. You decide late, when the facts are in front of you. Schedules and long plans tend to feel like a cost. You usually keep your thoughts to yourself until someone asks.\n\n## Strengths\n\nYou tend to stay level when something breaks and to go straight to what can be done. You notice how the parts of a thing fit together. You adapt quickly, because you were not attached to a plan to begin with. You usually say only what is needed, and what you say is accurate.\n\n## Blind spots\n\nYou may leave decisions and paperwork until they become urgent. You can miss that someone wanted a response from you and not a fix. Long-range consequences may get less attention than the problem in front of you. You may go quiet in a conflict and leave the other person guessing.\n\n## At work\n\nYou tend to want hands-on problems, room to work your own way and little supervision. You are likely to prefer doing the task over talking about it. Fixed routines, long meetings and rules that slow down an obvious fix tend to wear on you. You usually work in bursts, hardest when something is at stake.\n\n## Under stress\n\nYou may withdraw and become blunt, or drop the task and go do something physical. You might put off the decision even longer. Space, a practical problem to solve and no pressure to explain how you feel usually help most.\n\n## How others may read you\n\nPeople may see you as calm, private and hard to read. Your short answers can come across as lack of interest. Those close to you tend to learn that you show up when something real needs doing. You may need to say what you are thinking a little sooner than feels natural.\n",
};
